A||N||7||desertlord||3.4 Conversion||||1||
Z||000000||desertlord||11-23-2001||04:55 PM||||I know that this has been discussed before and I did search the archives but I want more comments.<BR>I am thinking of getting a Fiero that needs an engine. It is an 86 V6. Rather than just put in a new 2.8 I was thinking of something a little better. I have heard that a 3.4 (pushrod) is a simple drop in job aside from the starter holes. Is this true? I need to be able to do it quickly and cheaply. Also, how much do low mileage 3.4's sell for?<P>------------------<BR>Rick Lord<BR>Desert Lord Enterprises<BR>http://www.desertlord.com<BR>http://www.kitkar.com||||reg||1
Z||000001||jeffndebrus||11-23-2001||04:57 PM||||I believe if you go to Ed Parks site you will find some good info.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||jch82||11-24-2001||01:21 AM||||Your best bet is to talk with Ed (in Alabama) or Bob (in Illinois). They both know a lot about the 3.4 swap. I have been doing some research and it costs about $1200-$1400 for a 3.4 crate engine. A low mileage one would be less.<P>You have to move the starter, there is an engine mount that has to be modified and everything from the heads up on the 2.8 has to be put on the 3.4. I think that is about it. <P>Email Ed or Bob though, Bob will want you to call him though. Everytime I email him, he tells me to call him so we can talk. Haven't got around to it yet though...<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jch82_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||11-24-2001||05:41 PM||||You may be able to get some information from Dan Campbell Pisa Corp. ( <A HREF="http://www.cybercars.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cybercars.com</A>  ) he is located in Phoenix,Az. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||11-24-2001||05:57 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jch82:<BR><B>...everything from the heads up on the 2.8 has to be put on the 3.4. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The Fiero 2.8 and 3.4 Camaro motors both use the same heads. Same size valves. The Fiero intake system will bolt on to the Camaro heads. <P>Here is Ed Parks take on the situation... <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34</A> <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||A||11-24-2001||08:46 PM||||Why the 3.4 but not the 3.8?  I guess the 3.8 doesn't bolt up as pretty as the 3.4, eh?||||reg||1
Z||000006||desertlord||11-25-2001||06:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by A:<BR><B>Why the 3.4 but not the 3.8?  I guess the 3.8 doesn't bolt up as pretty as the 3.4, eh?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>3.8 is a much more expensive conversion.<P>------------------<BR>Rick Lord<BR>Desert Lord Enterprises<BR>http://www.desertlord.com<BR>http://www.kitkar.com||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||11-25-2001||09:25 PM||||The 3.8 (actually, 3800) is a 90* V-6. The 2.8 and 3.4 are 60* designs.<BR>The 3800 requires mounts to be fabricated, and also cannot use the Fiero ECM. Although the tranny bolt patterns are the same, the 3800 is not available in FWD configuration with a manual tranny. Requires a custom flywheel. I think some people have used the 3800 Camaro flywheel. Don't know the results, though. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
